<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2>Landed since your last approval</h2>
<p>You read this page's items up to item 19 at 16:49 UTC on 5 November 2026, so they left the page.</p>
<p>Four items, 20 to 23, each a change to the coordinator skill that you have not read. Commit <code>9449ed5aa</code>, at 16:53 UTC, made the changes that your comments of 16:38 to 16:49 on this page asked for. A cold read of it, read as the coordinating session, found ten problems, and at 16:58 the skill writer's commit <code>5dd90f9fe</code> fixed the text. Each item shows the net change of both commits, against the text before them.</p>
<p>In each item, the words that changed are underlined, and the words that went are struck through. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on its item, or say so in chat. The skill writer then restores the old words.</p>

<section id="l20" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 20 &middot; skill-rewrite.md, “The method”, step 7, “Landed changes” and “Proposed changes”</span>The page holds only what you have not read</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
<p class="where-in">skill-rewrite.md, “The method”, step 7, lines 20 to 26</p>
<ul><li>Landed changes: <span class="cut">one item for each change that landed since the curator's last approval on the page, or since the curator last said that they had read it,</span> <span class="add">only the landed changes that the curator has not read,</span> so that no change lands unseen while the curator is away. <span class="cut">On the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes, which holds no approvals, the items start from the curator's last word that they have read it.</span> <span class="add">The items stand oldest first, numbered on from the page's last item, so every item that the curator finds there is new:</span>
<ul><li><span class="add">If the curator says, on the page or in chat, that they have read the page up to an item, the next update removes that item and every older item. Write that item and the time of the word in the boot note's “Pages” line of that page. The page worker and the record-landing workflow's page stage both read it there. You may have the update made at once, without waiting for the next landing.</span></li><li><span class="add">A draft is an item under “Proposed changes” that waits for the curator's yes. A whole part of <code>fortress-repo</code> approved on its own page (step 4) is a draft too. A draft that the curator approved gets no item when it lands, unless the landing changed the approved words. A cold read's fix that changes no meaning, such as a path or a clearer word, lands, and its item shows that change. A fix that changes the meaning does not land: the draft goes back under “Proposed changes” for a new yes.</span></li><li><span class="add">Every other item stays until the curator says, on the page or in chat, that they have read it.</span></li></ul>
<p>The item takes the form of a landed item in <code>SKILL.md</code>, “Pages”. <span class="cut">For a change that waited for the curator's yes, it shows the change against the words that the curator approved. For any other change, it shows</span> <span class="add">It shows</span> the change against the skill's text <span class="cut">before it.</span> <span class="add">before it, or, for an approved draft whose words changed at the landing, against the approved words.</span> It gives the change's reason, which may come from a cold read, a hit of the skills' lint, <code>explorations/coordinator/tools/skills-lint.py</code>, or the record entry that the orientation workflow, in update mode, took in. The reason says in plain words what was wrong and what the text now says. It never cites a cold read's flag by its code or number, a judge's ruling by its number, or an item of a task by its number.</p></li>
<li>Proposed changes: the items that wait for the curator's yes. An approved item leaves this section when it <span class="cut">lands, and appears under the landed changes.</span> <span class="add">lands.</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The page had grown to nineteen items, and you could not tell which of them you had read; your comment of 16:49. The old text kept every change since your last approval or your last word that you had read the page, so the items piled up. Now the page holds only the changes that you have not read, and your word that you read up to an item takes that item and every older one off the page.</p>
<p>The first commit left five gaps, which the cold read found and the second commit closed. It did not say in what order the items stand, so “the items before it” could mean the newer ones; now they stand oldest first, numbered on from the last. It named no place to keep your word until the next update, and the workflow that updates the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes had no way to learn it; now the coordinator writes the item and the time of your word in the boot note's line for that page, where the page worker and the workflow both read it, and the update may be made at once. It gave an approved draft whose words changed at its landing two texts to be shown against; now there is one, the approved words. It did not say whether such a change of words may land at all, when an approval is a yes to exact words; now a cold read's fix that changes no meaning lands and is shown, and a fix that changes the meaning sends the draft back for a new yes. And “draft” was not defined, nor was “read”; now a draft is an item that waits for your yes, a whole part of <code>fortress-repo</code> approved on its own page included, and you read an item when you say so, on the page or in chat.</p>
<p>An approved item no longer moves to the landed changes when it lands, since you read its words when you approved it. The old sentence for the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes went, since the general rule now covers that page.</p></div>
</section>

